That choice of sourcing from an established high volume plant also surfaces when you look at how Tesla is handling

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.120
autonomy certification in Texas.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:43.900
Under state rules, manufacturers can self-approved autonomous systems for vehicles already in robot taxi testing.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:49.620
And Model Y units previously used in that program now carry the level 4 designation.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:54.740
The self-certification route shortens the usual federal review timeline,

00:02:55.220 --> 00:03:00.460
which means other states are watching to see how Texas enforcement actually plays out in practice.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:07.360
It's a regulatory step that directly affects how quickly those same Model Ys could move from supervised operation

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.000
to unsupervised commercial service inside the state.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:19.480
Even as certification moves forward in one jurisdiction, routine manufacturing details can still trigger formal recalls.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:28.640
Tesla recently called back nearly 15,000 Model Y vehicles because an automated applicator had skipped the required certification labels on some units.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.880
The issue is caught by internal quality checks before any vehicles reached customers,

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:38.460
and owners are being offered free inspections and label corrections at service centers.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:48.000
The episode underscores how even a single skipped step in a high volume process can still require regulatory follow-through and service center time.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:56.520
At the same time, Tesla is addressing those quality details at scale, where continues on the dedicated charging network needed for the semi.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:01.040
New high power sites are advancing along major Texas freight corridors,

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.820
positioned to serve early fleet operators ahead of wider commercial rollout.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:10.700
The next visible milestones are the completion dates for the first corridor segments,

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:17.920
which will determine how soon operators can plan regular routes that rely on those chargers rather than public infrastructure.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:23.600
